WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.240
A show's villain is as important as its hero, but&nbsp;
that doesn't mean we always know who the bad guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:12.320
is right away. Here’s a spoiler-filled&nbsp;
list of the best bad guy in TV history.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.080
When "Lost" arrived in 2004, there wasn't much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.960
like it on the small screen. It featured an&nbsp;
enormous cast and brought over 18 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:26.000
viewers to ABC — the highest ratings for an&nbsp;
offering on the network in almost four years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.240
One thing the show didn't have, however,&nbsp;
was an honest-to-goodness villain.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:36.000
The survivors of downed Oceanic Flight 815 had&nbsp;
to weather some pretty singular adversities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.360
There was the smoke monster, of course, and the&nbsp;
everlasting conflict between anti-hero Sawyer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.880
and, well, everyone else. But "Lost" was&nbsp;
missing a true big bad. All that changed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.320
however, when actor Michael Emerson showed&nbsp;
up as the mysterious Henry Gale in Season 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.560
Some of the members of the group felt Henry's&nbsp;
stories about arriving on the island in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:02.196
hot-air balloon, and losing his wife in the&nbsp;
subsequent crash, were a bit too neat to be true.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.920
So in the Season 2 episode "Lockdown," the Oceanic&nbsp;
survivors go looking for answers. They find a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:14.640
balloon and a grave, but Henry's wife isn't in it&nbsp;
— the real Henry is. When the phony Gale is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.840
out for the falsehood, Emerson really shines&nbsp;
in the role. He seems to morph into an entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:26.000
different person, Ben Linus, in the blink of an&nbsp;
eye. This manipulative antagonist would go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.960
to wreak havoc on the island in the following&nbsp;
seasons, finally giving "Lost" a true villain.

00:01:31.676 --> 00:01:35.920
The inaugural season of "True Detective"&nbsp;
follows two investigators — played by Matthew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.320
McConaughey [muh-KAH-nuh-hay] and Woody Harrelson&nbsp;
— on the hunt for a serial killer. Throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.360
season, the show's writer and director expertly&nbsp;
build the expectation of the evil we'll encounter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.640
when we meet our villain. The protagonists'&nbsp;
escalating run-ins with voodoo, kidnapping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.680
cover-ups, and violent meth dealers signal that&nbsp;
whatever is waiting at the end won't be pretty.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.520
At the end of Season 1 Episode 7, audiences&nbsp;
are finally clued into the killer's identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.320
and it's an almost forgettable&nbsp;
groundskeeper from way back in Episode 3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.480
In the hands of a lesser creative team, the&nbsp;
moment might've felt like a lazy callback.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.040
Not so here. By the time deranged killer&nbsp;
Errol Childress [AIR-uhl CHILL-driss] appears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.880
casually giving two other police&nbsp;
officers directions from his lawn mower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:24.400
viewers have witnessed a laundry list of evil&nbsp;
deeds. The dread of confronting something much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:29.760
worse than what the two leads have previously&nbsp;
encountered is as thick as Louisiana's humidity.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.160
As Errol watches the police officers drive&nbsp;
off and the creepy music starts to play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.720
we see scars on the lower part of his face&nbsp;
consistent with descriptions of a suspect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.240
Revelatory moments like this one really marked&nbsp;
the slow-burning 1st season of "True Detective"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.680
for greatness. The closing moments of "After&nbsp;
You've Gone" dare the audience to keep watching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.175
and almost make viewers hope Harrelson&nbsp;
and McConaughney stay at home.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.885
“Stop. Really, man. Stop. I don’t wanna hear it.”

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.720
For much of "The Flash" Season 1,&nbsp;
Harrison Wells acts as the kindly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:07.840
genius founder of S.T.A.R. [“star”] Labs. He&nbsp;
supports Barry Allen's efforts as a superhero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.000
and serves as his mentor. Over the course of&nbsp;
the season, however, his character begins to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.440
act increasingly strange, and Flash and his&nbsp;
respective allies suspect something is off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.600
A stinger in Episode 9 shows the audience&nbsp;
that Harrison is in possession of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.360
Reverse Flash's iconic suit, but the show&nbsp;
doesn't confirm the worst until Episode 15.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.800
By that point, most of Team Flash suspect Wells&nbsp;
is the yellow speedster, but not everyone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.720
convinced. Unfortunately, Wells' surrogate&nbsp;
son Cisco [SIS-coh] finds out the hard way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.320
After Cisco discovers where Wells&nbsp;
hides the suit in the previous episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.880
Wells confronts him, admitting that he not&nbsp;
only had a hand in Barry's mother's death but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:54.000
is actually Eobard [EE-oh-bard] Thawne — Flash's&nbsp;
all-time big bad. Anyone wondering why Wells might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.320
announce his secrets so bluntly gets their answer&nbsp;
when Wells follows his monologue by killing Cisco.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:05.760
This moment, where Thawne admits his sins and&nbsp;
kills a character his alter ego cared about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.120
cements the villain's full arrival and the scope&nbsp;
of his malevolence. And it happened in the 1st&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.640
episode back from the show's mid-season break,&nbsp;
giving fans a compelling reason to keep tuning in.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:21.360
Marvel's "WandaVision" kicks off when the&nbsp;
Scarlet Witch finds herself married to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:27.200
newly-risen Vision in an idyllic, sitcom-style&nbsp;
existence. Viewers watch as Wanda tries to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.640
embrace her new life, but slowly discovers&nbsp;
something's amiss. All isn't what it appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.095
to be in Wanda's primetime network town,&nbsp;
especially when it comes to her neighbors.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.520
Throughout the series, Wanda has kooky and&nbsp;
friendly encounters with a neighbor named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:45.840
Agnes [AG-ness], played by a scenery-chewing&nbsp;
Kathryn Hahn. Like any sitcom neighbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.080
she's a bit snoopy, but mostly kind&nbsp;
— until the end of Episode 7. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.800
Wanda takes a day to recharge, Agnes&nbsp;
looks after her children. Soon, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.120
Wanda suspects she and her family are&nbsp;
in danger and goes to collect her kids.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.101
“Where are the twins?”

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.680
Agnes tells Wanda the kids are playing in&nbsp;
the basement. When Wanda descends the stairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.920
she finds a witch's lair. Agnes then&nbsp;
walks into the room and introduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.320
herself as a fellow witch named&nbsp;
Agatha [AG-uh-thuh] Harkness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:18.400
As Agnes invades Wanda's mind, the show&nbsp;
suddenly cuts to the song "Agatha All Along."

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.120
In one catchy tune, Agatha is revealed to have&nbsp;
been pulling Wanda's strings throughout the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:29.200
series — and worse, she killed Wanda's dog. The&nbsp;
number is a great showcase for Hahn's charisma and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:34.640
enormously raises the stakes for the coming&nbsp;
series climax by introducing the main antagonist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.373 --> 00:05:38.129
Who needs subtle reveals?&nbsp;
More theme songs, please.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:44.960
When "Westworld" first premiered in 2016, its&nbsp;
robo-cowboy worldbuilding and unique storytelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:50.640
made it must-see TV. As the show confidently&nbsp;
swaggered through its time-bending opening season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:56.720
it delivered twist after twist. One of the most&nbsp;
devastating was the finale's bad guy reveal.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.400
In the show's premiere, audiences are&nbsp;
introduced to Jimmi Simpson's William&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:06.560
and Ed Harris' Man in Black. On first impressions,&nbsp;
the two stand in stark contrast to one another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.680
William is a meek but kind first-timer in&nbsp;
the theme park, and the Man in Black is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:17.520
park veteran who revels in doing horrible things&nbsp;
to its animatronic hosts. As the series unfolds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.480
it becomes clear that the show's story is&nbsp;
set in two distinct timelines. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:27.600
since "Westworld" was co-created by Jonathan&nbsp;
Nolan, brother and collaborator to the notoriously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:33.040
confusing director Christopher Nolan, it's not&nbsp;
always clear exactly which timeline is which.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:37.200
The show sets the record straight when&nbsp;
its protagonist, robotic host Dolores,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.360
played by Evan Rachel Wood, encounters&nbsp;
the Man in Black in the series finale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.080
He reveals that they've met before and that&nbsp;
his first encounter with Dolores helped him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:51.440
find himself. Dolores remembers who he is,&nbsp;
calling him William, and the editors intercut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.480
his monologue with sequences that show the&nbsp;
once-gentle William becoming the evil man he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:01.680
is now. It's tragic that someone so kind&nbsp;
could wind up so vile. But in a pivotal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:06.753
moment to the show, his gutting reveal clearly&nbsp;
establishes what's present and what's past.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.000
Not many detective shows leave audiences&nbsp;
as shaken as "Broadchurch" did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.000
and all of that is due to its villain&nbsp;
reveal. In the show, detectives Ellie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.560
Miller and Alec Hardy are investigating&nbsp;
the murder of 11-year-old named Danny.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.000
Like most detective shows, in the&nbsp;
end, the partners get their man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.080
but by time it's over audiences and&nbsp;
protagonists alike will wish the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.640
villain had never been revealed. The show&nbsp;
builds up our trust in relationships by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.754
dedicating notable attention to the supportive&nbsp;
relationship between Ellie and her husband Joe.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:42.960
So in the end, when Hardy discovers Ellie's&nbsp;
husband Joe is in possession of Danny's phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:49.040
anybody watching prays it's a red herring.&nbsp;
But it's not. A flashback reveals that Joe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:53.600
murdered Danny because Danny threatened to&nbsp;
expose their pedophilic affair to the town.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:59.453
It's awful on so many levels and adds real&nbsp;
bite to the series' focus on trust and secrets.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.760
The murder scene's frantic editing showcases&nbsp;
just how fast the threat of exposure escalates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:10.960
things between Joe and his young victim. Once&nbsp;
they find out what happened, viewers sit with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:16.080
turning stomachs waiting for Ellie to discover&nbsp;
her husband's secret. Only such a powerfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:21.200
depressing reveal can make the audience hope&nbsp;
one of the protagonists never learns the truth.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.280
The HBO series "Watchmen" has a bad guy reveal&nbsp;
that is quite different from it’s graphic novel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:31.680
bad guy reveal. When viewers first meet&nbsp;
Lady Trieu [TROO], she's a brilliant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:35.600
conglomerate-owning, clone-maintaining&nbsp;
scientist with a hidden agenda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:40.240
However, it's not clear just how dark&nbsp;
that agenda is until it's too late.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.160
At first, the show's big bad appears to be an&nbsp;
organization called Cyclops [SYE-klahps], a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:47.920
white supremacist group out to&nbsp;
steal Dr. Manhattan's powers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.080
However, when they attempt their plot in the&nbsp;
finale, their leader gets turned into a puddle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:57.120
and his followers are teleported away to find&nbsp;
Trieu waiting with an even larger evil plan.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:01.520
Trieu reveals herself as the real puppet-master&nbsp;
and the daughter of Adrian Viedt [VITE], the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:05.280
surprise villain in the comics. It's&nbsp;
brilliant because it not only connects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.000
Trieu to a character from the source material,&nbsp;
but also connects their murderous motives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.890
In the comics, Adrian blew up Manhattan to&nbsp;
trick the world into agreeing to world peace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:20.240
In the HBO series, Trieu murders Dr. Manhattan&nbsp;
in an attempt to subjugate the world to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:25.680
benevolent will. The comic and TV series&nbsp;
are separated by decades of cultural change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:29.760
but the latter's villain reveal unites&nbsp;
their central questions about wielding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.160
immense power over other people in the&nbsp;
service of a personal utopian vision.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:41.200
Humanizing a murderer is tricky in any medium,&nbsp;
but Showtime's "Dexter" managed to do it for eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:46.560
seasons — and was eventually revived for a ninth.&nbsp;
The show's premise, that Dexter is a serial killer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:51.920
who only kills other serial killers, is a strong&nbsp;
way to hook an audience. As the show goes on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:57.120
however, its elaborate twists and turns&nbsp;
prove it's more than just its engaging hook.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.480
During Season 1, the show fleshes out Dexter's&nbsp;
healthy relationship with his adopted sister, Deb.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:07.280
It also establishes a rivalry between himself&nbsp;
and a serial killer known only as the Ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:12.160
Truck Killer. So things get complicated&nbsp;
when the audience discovers that the man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:16.880
courting Deb is actually — gasp&nbsp;
— the Ice Truck Killer himself.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:21.760
In a reveal so casual it demands an immediate&nbsp;
re-watch, Deb discusses plans for a date with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:26.880
her suitor Rudy. Rudy tells Deb he needs to&nbsp;
wrap up some housework before coming over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.840
After he hangs up the phone,&nbsp;
the audience gets a look at what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:35.280
that work is — chopping and packing human&nbsp;
body parts in his walk-in murder freezer.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:40.480
It's an impeccable dramatic irony. The viewers&nbsp;
know the truth about Rudy, but Deb and Dexter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:45.200
remain in the dark — for a long time! For the&nbsp;
remainder of the season, the audience watches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:51.520
Dexter and Deb inch closer to the truth — and the&nbsp;
danger, and the tension is tangible. "Dexter" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:56.640
engrossing entertainment, and moments like&nbsp;
these cement its place in great TV history.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:00.960
The villain reveal at the end of&nbsp;
HBO's "Sharp Objects" is probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.920
the most audacious on this list, casting the&nbsp;
events of the entire show in a new light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:10.160
"Sharp Objects" is based on Gillian&nbsp;
Flynn's novel of the same name — yep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:16.320
the same author behind "Gone Girl," another story&nbsp;
with a shocking villain reveal. HBO's miniseries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:20.800
follows crime reporter Camille Preaker as&nbsp;
she returns home to Wind Gap, Missouri.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:25.440
There, she gets caught up investigating the&nbsp;
murders of some local girls. In between sleuthing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:30.800
she navigates her complex relationships with&nbsp;
her mother, Adora, and teen half-sister, Amma.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:35.680
Ultimately, Camille discovers evidence suggesting&nbsp;
Adora killed the girls and sends her own mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:41.360
to prison. Bleak stuff. But this is a Gillian&nbsp;
Flynn story we're talking about. It can always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:46.640
get a lot worse in the most unexpected ways,&nbsp;
and moments before the credits roll, it does.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:51.520
Literally within the show's closing minutes,&nbsp;
Camille discovers evidence hidden in Amma's room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:57.360
that suggests she was the killer all along. Amma&nbsp;
appears in the doorway, sees Camille, and whispers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:02.160
"Don't tell Mama." The show hard-cuts to the&nbsp;
credits, but anyone trying to catch their breath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:08.000
is acting too soon. A mid-credits scene shows Amma&nbsp;
definitively committing each murder, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:14.320
that of a new friend, Mae, some time later after&nbsp;
she and Camille move to St. Louis. It's brilliant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:19.120
and possibly one of the most depressing&nbsp;
endings ever committed to the small screen.

